WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:06.500
Speaker 1: One of the biggest flashpoints
in the 2016 presidential election came around

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:15.349
records, records, retention emails, email
servers, responsibility with classified and

00:00:15.349 --> 00:00:20.180
sensitive information all directed at Hillary
Clinton.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:24.110
Remember that this was the biggest storyline
about Hillary Clinton.

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:25.789
She would be in jail, Trump said.

00:00:25.789 --> 00:00:29.710
If I was president because of what she did
with emails and documents and so on and so

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:31.539
forth every day.

00:00:31.539 --> 00:00:32.980
Now I'm not exaggerating.

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:38.220
Every day, just over the weekend, each day
there was another major story about the Trump

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.040
White House and records.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.020
And we have we are going to look at two of
those stories now.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:51.329
And the first is that essentially a raid took
place on Mar a Lago, where the National Archives

00:00:51.329 --> 00:00:58.309
showed up to get Trump's documents, many of
which related to meetings with North Korean

00:00:58.309 --> 00:00:59.930
dictator Kim Jong UN.

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:01.360
Remember those love letters?

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:05.180
Remember, Trump said when we fell in love?

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:10.340
Trump did not turn over the records to the
National Archives.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:14.470
He took them to Mar a Lago, and the National
Archives had to go down there and get the

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:15.749
records.

00:01:15.749 --> 00:01:18.600
This is an incredible story.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:25.429
Washington Post reporters Jacqueline Alemany,
Josh Dawsey and others reporting President

00:01:25.429 --> 00:01:30.909
Trump improperly removed multiple boxes from
the White House that were retrieved by the

00:01:30.909 --> 00:01:34.740
National Archives last month from his Mar
a Lago residence.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:37.299
He just took the documents home.

00:01:37.299 --> 00:01:40.869
Because they contain documents and other items
that should have been turned over to the agency,

00:01:40.869 --> 00:01:44.009
according to three people familiar with the
visit.

00:01:44.009 --> 00:01:48.869
The recovery of the boxes from Trump's Florida
resort raises new concerns about his adherence

00:01:48.869 --> 00:01:54.119
to the Presidential Records Act, which requires
the preservation of memos, letters, notes,

00:01:54.119 --> 00:01:59.520
emails, faxes and other written communications
related to a president's official duties.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:05.599
Trump advisers deny any nefarious intent,
of course, and say the boxes contain mementos,

00:02:05.599 --> 00:02:13.970
gifts, letters from world leaders who could
not realize that those are presidential records

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:16.000
and other correspondence.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:21.030
The items included correspondence with North
Korean leader Kim Jong UN, which Trump once

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:23.200
described as love letters.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.680
Speaker 2: And then we fell in love.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:26.680
Right, right.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:31.490
Speaker 1: As well as a letter left for his
successor by President Barack Obama, according

00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:33.900
to two people familiar with the contents.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:38.409
Discussions between the archives and the former
president's lawyers that began last year resulted

00:02:38.409 --> 00:02:41.130
in the transfer of the records in January.

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:44.890
Another person familiar with the materials
said Trump advisers discussed what had to

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:47.239
be returned in December.

00:02:47.239 --> 00:02:50.220
People familiar with the transfer spoke on
the condition of anonymity.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:58.359
So in December, they explained to Trump what
needed to be returned, and it took the National

00:02:58.359 --> 00:03:01.340
Archives showing up later to actually get
the stuff.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:04.629
The archives have struggled to cope with a
president who flouted.

00:03:04.629 --> 00:03:05.629
There it is, guys.

00:03:05.629 --> 00:03:06.900
See, I told you it's a real word.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:09.440
People always say when I say, Flouted David,
don't you mean flaunt it?

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:11.819
And I say no, I mean flouted.

00:03:11.819 --> 00:03:17.040
A president who flouted document retention
requirements and frequently ripped up official

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:21.249
documents, leaving hundreds of pages taped
back together.

00:03:21.249 --> 00:03:28.360
So this is this story, and we're going to
talk about the incineration of documents in

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:29.530
a moment.

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:35.549
This is the prototypical backwards projection
scam that was this entire presidency.

00:03:35.549 --> 00:03:40.500
The story in 2016 was Hillary's going to do
bad stuff with records.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:46.469
Hillary can't be trusted with email documents
classified, secret sensitive and since Trump

00:03:46.469 --> 00:03:48.069
has left.

00:03:48.069 --> 00:03:53.470
We found out that the real concern around
all of those things was Donald Trump and his

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:56.060
kids and other people that worked for him.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:04:00.900
Do we think Hillary would have eaten documents
as Donald Trump allegedly did?

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:01.900
No.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:06.520
Do we think Hillary would have ripped documents
and tossed them onto the floor and then people

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:09.779
run in and grab the scraps and tape them back
together?

00:04:09.779 --> 00:04:10.879
Of course not.

00:04:10.879 --> 00:04:15.280
Do we think Hillary would have taken documents
about foreign policy meetings and letters

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:17.660
from foreign leaders to her house?

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:22.199
Give me a break, guys, and we can argue about
anything else about Hillary versus Trump.

00:04:22.199 --> 00:04:23.199
That's fine.

00:04:23.199 --> 00:04:27.449
But on this issue, which was the flash point
about why Hillary can't be trusted, Trump's

00:04:27.449 --> 00:04:30.550
the guy that we need to worry about.

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:34.039
I would love to hear more about the retrieval
that took place.

00:04:34.039 --> 00:05:12.800
And this is not the only story about Trump
and White House records that 

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:40.800
we 

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:41.000
have today.

